@startuml
title SeparatorOrganicizer (revised) - find LOCAL corridor segments instead of gating on a whole component's global shape

start
:Scan every cell in the region;

if (bounded run on SOME axis (contiguous\nsame biome, capped on BOTH ends by a real,\ndifferent, MUTUALLY-INCOMPATIBLE-WITH-\nEACH-OTHER biome pair) is\n<= CORRIDOR_WIDTH_THRESHOLD?) then (no)
  :Not locally corridor-shaped - skip;
else (yes)
  :Mark this cell as a "corridor cell";
endif

:Cluster all corridor cells\n(4-connected, same biome only -\nConnectedComponents.find restricted\nto just this subset, same pattern\nSeamWidener already uses for pinch\nclusters);

while (clusters left to process?) is (yes)
  :Bulge this cluster - unchanged logic,\njust now applied to a small LOCAL\ncluster instead of the whole\nconnected component;
endwhile (no)

stop

note right
  The bug this replaces: the OLD version measured
  isElongated()/isRequiredSeparator() on the cell's WHOLE
  connected component. Fine for a small, genuinely
  wall-shaped separator patch - wrong for a biome like
  plains, which can sprawl into one 800+-cell component
  spanning nearly the entire map by connecting through
  many regions. That component's overall bounding-box
  aspect ratio reads as roughly square (blobby elsewhere),
  so isElongated() returned false and bulge() never ran
  AT ALL - even though a specific middle-region stretch of
  it measured a perfectly uniform 3-4 cells wide across 16
  sampled rows: a straight corridor, zero organic variation,
  hiding inside a component that looked "fine" in aggregate.

  This version measures shape LOCALLY per cell instead of
  globally per component - a corridor segment gets found
  and organicized regardless of what the rest of that
  biome's sprawling territory looks like elsewhere.

  Runs after SeparatorWidthEnforcer (so corridors are
  already >= 3 wide going in - this pass only affects
  organic variation, not the hard minimum) using the same
  AxisRunFinder bounded-run measurement SeparatorWidthEnforcer
  already uses, and the same bulge() steal mechanism this
  class already had (legality/connectivity/min-size
  checked per steal, same as everywhere else in this
  pipeline) - only the CLUSTER SELECTION changed, not how
  a cluster gets bulged once found.
end note
@enduml
